1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,681
This is MATH 239, video 12. The topic is binary strings, regular expressions, and ambiguity. It covers sections 3.1 to 3.2.1 of the course notes.

2
00:00:11,131 --> 00:00:30,497
After this video, you can read a regular expression and list the strings that it produces. You can decide if an expression is ambiguous, and prove it. You can turn an unambiguous expression into a generating series. And you can solve practice problem P4-1 and Practice Midterm question 6, parts a and b.

3
00:00:30,947 --> 00:00:39,074
A binary string is a finite sequence of bits. Each bit is 0 or 1. The number of bits is the length of the string.

4
00:00:39,524 --> 00:00:53,781
There is exactly one string of length 0. It is the empty string, epsilon. Length 1 gives two strings, 0 and 1. The set of all binary strings is the union of the sets of strings of length n, over all n.

5
00:00:54,231 --> 00:00:56,274
Length 2 gives four strings.

6
00:00:56,724 --> 00:01:04,619
Length 3 gives eight strings. In general, there are 2 to the n strings of length n, because each of the n bits has two choices.

7
00:01:05,069 --> 00:01:17,097
So the generating series of all binary strings, with respect to length, is the sum of 2 to the n x to the n. That is one over one minus 2x. In this chapter, the weight of a string is always its length.

8
00:01:17,547 --> 00:01:37,005
A regular expression is defined recursively. Epsilon, 0 and 1 are regular expressions. If R and S are regular expressions, then R or S is one, and R S, the concatenation, is one. R to the k means k copies of R. And R star is a regular expression.

9
00:01:37,455 --> 00:01:45,536
Here is the expression 1 star, 0 1 star, star. Its top operation is a concatenation of two parts.

10
00:01:45,986 --> 00:01:49,933
The first part is 1 star. The 1 at the bottom is a basic expression.

11
00:01:50,383 --> 00:02:00,878
The second part is the star of 0 1 star. Inside it, 0 is concatenated with 1 star. Each node of the tree uses one rule of the definition.

12
00:02:01,328 --> 00:02:10,912
The symbol that looks like a cup means or. An expression is only a formal construction. We read it in two ways: as a set of strings, and as a rational function.

13
00:02:11,362 --> 00:02:32,832
The concatenation of alpha and beta is the bits of alpha followed by the bits of beta. For two sets of strings, A B is the set of all concatenations alpha beta, with alpha in A and beta in B. Example 3.4 uses A equals 011 and 01, and B equals 101 and 1101.

14
00:02:33,282 --> 00:02:41,827
First pair: 011 then 101. The orange cells come from A. The purple line marks the cut.

15
00:02:42,277 --> 00:02:44,599
011 then 1101.

16
00:02:45,049 --> 00:02:47,928
01 then 101.

17
00:02:48,378 --> 00:02:51,165
01 then 1101.

18
00:02:51,615 --> 00:03:04,897
Look at the two red boxes. 011 dot 101 and 01 dot 1101 give the same string, 011101. The result does not record where the cut was.

19
00:03:05,347 --> 00:03:16,213
So A B has only 3 strings, but A cross B has 4 pairs. The concatenation product is not the Cartesian product. This fact is the source of ambiguity.

20
00:03:16,663 --> 00:03:31,013
Now the first reading. Each expression produces a set of strings. Epsilon, 0 and 1 produce the sets with that one string. As an example, let R produce 0 and 10, and let S produce 1.

21
00:03:31,463 --> 00:03:37,176
R or S produces the union of the two sets: 0, 10, and 1.

22
00:03:37,626 --> 00:03:42,687
R S produces the concatenation product: 01 and 101.

23
00:03:43,137 --> 00:03:51,125
R star produces the union of all R to the k. R to the 0 is the empty string. R to the 1 is R itself.

24
00:03:51,575 --> 00:03:59,413
R to the 2 has four strings: 00, 010, 100, and 1010.

25
00:03:59,863 --> 00:04:09,987
The union of all these layers is R star. A set of strings that some regular expression produces is called a rational language. Many different expressions can produce the same set.

26
00:04:10,437 --> 00:04:14,385
1 star produces all strings of 1s, including the empty string.

27
00:04:14,835 --> 00:04:22,371
1 or 11, star, produces the same set. Each string of 1s can be built from pieces 1 and 11.

28
00:04:22,821 --> 00:04:30,530
1 then 11 star produces the strings of 1s of odd length: 1, 111, 11111, and so on.

29
00:04:30,980 --> 00:04:37,528
0 or 1, star, produces every binary string. Each piece is one bit.

30
00:04:37,978 --> 00:04:46,477
1 star, 0 1 star, star, also produces every binary string. So two different expressions can produce the same rational language.

31
00:04:46,927 --> 00:04:54,496
01 star produces the empty string, 01, 0101, and so on. It is a rational language.

32
00:04:54,946 --> 00:04:59,590
The second set has j zeros followed by j ones, for each j.

33
00:05:00,040 --> 00:05:06,310
Both sets have exactly one string of each even length. So they have the same generating series.

34
00:05:06,760 --> 00:05:22,874
But the second set is not a rational language. To produce it, we would need the union over all j of 0 to the j, 1 to the j. That is an infinite union. Definition 3.2 allows only finitely many operations, so it does not allow this.

35
00:05:23,324 --> 00:05:34,516
An expression is unambiguous if it produces every string of its set exactly once. Otherwise it is ambiguous. Ambiguity is a property of the expression, not of the set.

36
00:05:34,966 --> 00:05:39,750
1 star produces 111 in one way: three copies of 1.

37
00:05:40,200 --> 00:05:47,119
1 or 11, star, also produces 111. One way is three pieces: 1, 1, 1.

38
00:05:47,569 --> 00:05:49,891
A second way is 1 then 11.

39
00:05:50,341 --> 00:06:01,626
A third way is 11 then 1. So this expression produces 111 three times. It is ambiguous, but it produces the same set as 1 star.

40
00:06:02,076 --> 00:06:12,107
Lemma 3.9 decides ambiguity one operation at a time. Assume R and S are unambiguous. First, epsilon, 0 and 1 are unambiguous.

41
00:06:12,557 --> 00:06:20,385
R or S is unambiguous if and only if the two sets are disjoint. If a string is in both sets, it is produced twice.

42
00:06:20,835 --> 00:06:39,039
R S is unambiguous if and only if each string in the product has exactly one cut into a string of R and a string of S. In Example 3.4, 011101 has two cuts. Then R S matches R cross S, one to one.

43
00:06:39,489 --> 00:06:48,080
R star is unambiguous if and only if each power R to the k is unambiguous, and no string is in two different powers.

44
00:06:48,530 --> 00:06:59,026
Now prove that 1 star, 0 1 star, star, is unambiguous. Take the string 1101001. The zeros are orange.

45
00:06:59,476 --> 00:07:10,296
Each piece 0 1 star starts with a 0, and has no other 0. So every piece must start exactly at a 0. Cut before every 0.

46
00:07:10,746 --> 00:07:23,796
The pieces are forced. 11 comes from 1 star. Then 01, 0, and 01 come from 0 1 star. Inside a piece, the number of 1s is fixed by the piece.

47
00:07:24,246 --> 00:07:40,639
The string has 3 zeros, so it uses exactly 3 pieces. Strings of different powers have different numbers of zeros, so the union over k is disjoint. The initial 1s are the 1s before the first 0. So each string is produced exactly once.

48
00:07:41,089 --> 00:07:48,102
0 or 1, star, is easier. Each piece is one bit, so the cuts are after every bit. There is only one way.

49
00:07:48,552 --> 00:08:03,180
The second reading. Each expression leads to a rational function. Epsilon leads to 1. 0 and 1 each lead to x. Or leads to a sum. Concatenation leads to a product. Star leads to one over one minus R of x.

50
00:08:03,630 --> 00:08:10,550
Work up the tree of 1 star, 0 1 star, star. Each bit is a leaf, and it leads to x.

51
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:13,693
1 star leads to one over one minus x.

52
00:08:14,143 --> 00:08:20,227
0 then 1 star leads to x times one over one minus x, which is x over one minus x.

53
00:08:20,677 --> 00:08:31,962
The star of that leads to one over one minus x over one minus x. Multiply top and bottom by one minus x. The result is one minus x, over one minus 2x.

54
00:08:32,412 --> 00:08:43,557
At the root, multiply the two parts. The one minus x cancels, and the result is one over one minus 2x. This is the generating series of all binary strings.

55
00:08:44,007 --> 00:08:56,778
Here are four expressions and their rational functions, with the coefficients. 1 star leads to one over one minus x. All coefficients are 1. That is correct: one string of 1s of each length.

56
00:08:57,228 --> 00:09:11,625
1 or 11, star, leads to one over one minus x minus x squared. The coefficients are 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8. But this expression produces only one string of each length. These numbers are wrong.

57
00:09:12,075 --> 00:09:21,316
0 or 1, star, leads to one over one minus 2x. The coefficients are the powers of 2. That is correct.

58
00:09:21,766 --> 00:09:30,311
1 star, 0 1 star, star, also leads to one over one minus 2x, as on the last slide. Also correct.

59
00:09:30,761 --> 00:09:41,907
Theorem 3.13. If R is an unambiguous expression, then its rational function equals the generating series of its set, with respect to length.

60
00:09:42,357 --> 00:09:50,530
The proof goes up the expression, one operation at a time. An unambiguous or is a disjoint union. So by the Sum Lemma, the series add.

61
00:09:50,980 --> 00:10:02,915
An unambiguous concatenation gives a bijection between R S and R cross S. The length of rho sigma is the length of rho plus the length of sigma. So by the Product Lemma, the series multiply.

62
00:10:03,365 --> 00:10:10,099
An unambiguous star satisfies the conditions of the String Lemma. So the series is one over one minus R of x.

63
00:10:10,549 --> 00:10:20,301
The basic expressions epsilon, 0 and 1 have series 1, x and x. By induction, the theorem holds for every unambiguous expression.

64
00:10:20,751 --> 00:10:29,389
The theorem needs the expression to be unambiguous. The set of all strings of 1s has series one over one minus x. Every coefficient is 1.

65
00:10:29,839 --> 00:10:50,969
Epsilon or 1, star, also produces this set. But each piece can be epsilon, so it produces each string in infinitely many ways. Its rational function is one over one minus one plus x, which is minus one over x. That says minus 1 objects of size minus 1. This makes no sense.

66
00:10:51,419 --> 00:11:00,615
1 or 11, star, gives one over one minus x minus x squared. The coefficients 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8 are wrong too.

67
00:11:01,065 --> 00:11:13,278
The coefficient of x cubed is 3. That is the number of ways the expression makes 111, as we saw. So a check of a few small coefficients against a direct count can catch an ambiguous expression.

68
00:11:13,728 --> 00:11:24,967
This slide is optional. It is not in the notes, but it explains the wrong numbers. The coefficient of x to the n in R of x counts the productions of length n, with repeats.

69
00:11:25,417 --> 00:11:32,011
Take 1111 in 1 or 11, star. One production is four pieces of 1.

70
00:11:32,461 --> 00:11:39,056
Three productions use one piece 11 and two pieces 1. One production uses two pieces 11.

71
00:11:39,506 --> 00:11:53,289
That is 5 productions, and the coefficient of x to the 4 is 5. When the expression is unambiguous, each string is produced once, so the coefficient counts strings. That is Theorem 3.13 again.

72
00:11:53,739 --> 00:12:08,321
On an exam, the answer has two forms. To prove that an expression is ambiguous, give one string and two different productions of it. For example, 00 in 0 or 00, star. This is a complete proof.

73
00:12:08,771 --> 00:12:29,338
To prove that an expression is unambiguous, explain why each string has only one production. Common arguments: read the string from left to right and show each piece is forced. Or show no piece is a prefix of another piece. Or count something, such as the number of zeros. Or show that the expression restricts a known unambiguous expression.

74
00:12:29,788 --> 00:12:49,246
Exercise 3.3 of the notes. A is 00 or 101 or 11. B is 00 or 001 or 10 or 110. Show that A star is unambiguous and B star is ambiguous. Then find the generating series of A star. Pause the video and try it.

75
00:12:49,696 --> 00:13:07,065
For A star, read from the left. If the next bit is 0, the piece must be 00. If it is 1, look at the second bit. 0 means the piece is 101. 1 means the piece is 11. No piece is a prefix of another piece, so each piece is forced.

76
00:13:07,515 --> 00:13:16,152
For example, 1100101 decodes in only one way: 11, then 00, then 101.

77
00:13:16,602 --> 00:13:29,048
A star is unambiguous, so by Theorem 3.13 its series is one over one minus x squared plus x cubed plus x squared. That is one over one minus 2 x squared minus x cubed.

78
00:13:29,498 --> 00:13:43,198
For B star, 00 is a prefix of 001. The string 00110 is 001 then 10, and also 00 then 110. Two productions, so B star is ambiguous.

79
00:13:43,648 --> 00:13:56,465
Practice problem P4-1, part a. Is 00 or 010, star, ambiguous? If it is not, find its generating series. Pause the video and try it.

80
00:13:56,915 --> 00:14:09,779
Read the string from the left. Each piece starts with 0. Look at the second bit. If it is 0, the piece is 00. If it is 1, the piece is 010. So each piece is forced.

81
00:14:10,229 --> 00:14:24,115
For example, 0 1 0 0 0 0 0 0 1 0 decodes as 0 1 0, then 0 0, then 0 0, then 0 1 0, and in no other way.

82
00:14:24,565 --> 00:14:42,630
So the expression is unambiguous. The two pieces lead to x squared and x cubed. By Theorem 3.13, the series is one over one minus x squared minus x cubed. The coefficients 1, 0, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3 match a direct count of the strings.

83
00:14:43,080 --> 00:14:48,513
Part b: 0 or 00, star. Pause the video and try it.

84
00:14:48,963 --> 00:14:55,233
The string 00 is produced as 0 then 0, and also as the single piece 00.

85
00:14:55,683 --> 00:15:00,048
So the expression is ambiguous. This one string is the full proof.

86
00:15:00,498 --> 00:15:15,498
Part c: 00 or 01 or 10 or 11, star. Pause the video and try it. Every piece has length 2, so the cuts must be after every second bit. Each string is produced once.

87
00:15:15,948 --> 00:15:27,605
So it is unambiguous. There are four pieces, each with series x squared. The series is one over one minus 4 x squared. There are 4 to the k strings of length 2k, and none of odd length.

88
00:15:28,055 --> 00:15:36,507
Practice Midterm question 6, part a. Is this expression ambiguous or unambiguous? Prove it. Pause the video and try it.

89
00:15:36,957 --> 00:15:50,742
Look for a short string that two factors can both make. The middle star can make 111: one piece, with zero copies of 11 and zero copies of 0000. Then the last factor gives epsilon.

90
00:15:51,192 --> 00:16:03,498
But the last factor can also make 111. 111 star is 11 followed by 1 star, so it gives 11, 111, 1111, and so on. Then the middle star uses zero pieces.

91
00:16:03,948 --> 00:16:13,794
So 111 has two productions. The expression is ambiguous. Write the answer like this: name the string, and write both productions factor by factor.

92
00:16:14,244 --> 00:16:21,813
Part b. We are told the expression is unambiguous. Find the generating series by length. Pause the video and try it.

93
00:16:22,263 --> 00:16:30,019
Translate from the bottom up. 011 then 111 star leads to x cubed times one over one minus x cubed.

94
00:16:30,469 --> 00:16:44,912
000 then 0 star leads to x cubed over one minus x. 1 then 1 star leads to x over one minus x. Their product is x to the 4 over the square of one minus x.

95
00:16:45,362 --> 00:16:49,309
The or adds the two parts. Call the sum P of x.

96
00:16:49,759 --> 00:16:59,418
The star leads to one over one minus P of x. The last factor, epsilon or 0 or 00, leads to one plus x plus x squared.

97
00:16:59,868 --> 00:17:25,782
Multiply. The answer is a fraction. The top is one plus x plus x squared. The bottom is one, minus x cubed over one minus x cubed, minus x to the 4 over the square of one minus x. By Theorem 3.13 this is the generating series. A direct count of the strings gives 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 4, 8, 13, and the series has the same coefficients.

98
00:17:26,232 --> 00:17:41,000
To recap. A regular expression is built from epsilon, 0 and 1 with or, concatenation and star. It produces a rational language. The concatenation product loses the cut point, so it is not the Cartesian product.

99
00:17:41,450 --> 00:17:57,750
An expression is unambiguous if it produces every string exactly once. Lemma 3.9 checks this one operation at a time. To prove ambiguity, give one string with two productions. To prove unambiguity, show that each piece is forced.

100
00:17:58,200 --> 00:18:13,275
Translate with: epsilon to 1, each bit to x, or to plus, concatenation to times, and star to one over one minus R of x. For an unambiguous expression, this gives the generating series by length. The next video covers block and prefix decompositions.
